Id: lin051w
Severity: WARN
Category: network
Check: check_network_config
Fix: Set net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1 and net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter=1 in /etc/sysctl.d, unless the host routes asymmetrically.
References: Linux kernel networking documentation (ip-sysctl, rp_filter); RFC 3704; CIS Benchmarks for Linux distributions, 3.3.7 (reverse path filtering)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST SC-5, SC-7; ISO 8.20; CE Secure configuration

Reverse path filtering is in loose mode (2): an incoming packet is
dropped only when no route at all leads back to its source address.
Strict mode (1) drops it unless the route back leaves through the
interface it arrived on, which is what RFC 3704 recommends for a host
with one way out and what CIS 3.3.7 asks for. The difference is a
spoofed source: in loose mode a packet from outside carrying an
address that belongs behind another interface (a container bridge, a
VPN, a second network) is accepted, and whatever trusts that address
by its source is fooled.

The kernel applies the higher of conf/all and the interface's own
value, so all and default must both be 1 for new interfaces, and on
the running system every interface is judged by its own value. The
finding names the interfaces concerned. systemd's 50-default.conf
(Fedora, Arch) and Ubuntu's 10-network-security.conf set 2, because
strict mode drops legitimate traffic where routing is asymmetric: the
reply leaves by another interface than the request came in on. Where
this host routes that way, keep loose mode and accept the finding.
Alpine ships 1; Debian sets nothing, so the kernel's 0 (lin014f).
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter=1
